WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.160
In Avengers: Endgame, the surviving heroes
wrestled with the reality that half of all

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.240
beings in the universe had been snapped out
of existence.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:09.900
But that's not all they wrestled with.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.520
Spoilers ahead, folks!

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.320
The trauma of the initial snap, plus the revelation
that Thanos had reduced the Infinity Stones

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.660
to atoms, affects each of Earth's Mightiest
in different ways.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:23.880
Most fans agree that Endgame depicted the
range of emotions in an excellent way.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:28.500
Unfortunately, there has been some serious
backlash over how one particular character

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.600
and his struggle with grief was portrayed
in the film.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.740
That's right: A lot of people are outraged
over "fat Thor" in Endgame.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:39.220
Avengers: Endgame saw the Asgardian god of
thunder Thor fight with overwhelming feelings

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:44.340
of failure, for not protecting Hulk, Loki,
and Heimdall at the start of Avengers: Infinity

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.840
War, and for not killing Thanos when he had
the chance to.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:51.220
Thor's guilt sent him to a dark place mentally
and emotionally, and he appeared after the

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:55.820
five-year time jump in Endgame having found
comfort in alcohol, Fortnite, and high-calorie

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:56.820
snacks.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:00.440
"Something I've been roasting in the sun for
a number of weeks now.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:02.580
It's almost ready."

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:07.020
He has a big beer belly, scraggly hair, and
a thick beard that would make lumberjacks

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.020
jealous.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.940
Most of all, Thor isn't as confident or self-assured
as he used to be, though he's still quite

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:12.940
chipper.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:17.340
Following that big reveal, several jokes are
made at Thor's expense throughout Endgame,

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:19.820
with characters poking fun at his robust figure.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:23.300
They joke that he looks like the Dude from
The Big Lebowski, quip that he didn't fall

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:27.530
asleep but actually died in the middle of
an important meeting, and jab that he has

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:29.560
Cheez Whiz coursing through his veins.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:34.119
While some giggled along with the gags, many
others found fault with it all, arguing that

00:01:34.119 --> 00:01:38.409
"fat Thor" in Avengers: Endgame is problematic,
as it encourages audiences to laugh at someone

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:42.869
who exhibits symptoms of depression, alcohol
dependence, and post-traumatic stress disorder.

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:45.000
Lacey-Jade Christie wrote at The Guardian:

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:08.720
Cosmopolitan's Emily Tannenbaum penned her
own piece about the new Thor, writing in part:

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.299
Many more took issue with "fat Thor" for a
variety of different reasons, but some offered

00:02:24.299 --> 00:02:25.870
rebuttals to the outrage.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.959
Slashfilm's Ethan Anderton was one such individual.

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:32.790
He published an opinion piece arguing that
Avengers: Endgame actually handles the hero's

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:34.300
personal journey quite well.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:55.959
In the wake of the backlash and the response
to that criticism, Avengers: Endgame screenwriters

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:00.220
Christopher Markus and Stephen McFeely spoke
to Vulture about their vision for Thor's physical

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:02.340
and emotional transformation in the film.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:06.860
Markus explained that he and McFeely didn't
want to put Thor "on a mission of vengeance,"

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:10.260
as that was basically his entire arc in Avengers:
Infinity War.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:13.600
"Bring me Thanos!"

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:18.919
Instead, they wondered what he would look,
act, and feel like after facing failure and

00:03:18.919 --> 00:03:20.449
losing family and friends.

00:03:20.449 --> 00:03:24.430
The screenwriter made it clear that there
was no ill intent in having Thor gain weight.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:25.420
Markus explained:

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:44.879
Hopefully, Marvel Studios can take the feedback
to the new Thor and use that to inform the

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.760
character's post-Endgame trajectory.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:50.300
Besides, it's not like Thor is the first character
to be the brunt of a few mean-spirited jokes

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:51.440
about his weight.

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:52.320
"I'm muscular."

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:53.280
"Who are you kidding, Quill?

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.220
You're one sandwich away from fat."

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:56.040
"Yeah, right."

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:57.000
"It's true.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:03:58.020
You have put on weight."

00:03:58.020 --> 00:03:58.880
"What?"

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:05.700
Thor seems poised to appear in the third Guardians
movie, so maybe he and Quill can both rock

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:07.700
the dad bod while zipping through space.

